[Event "01 The Taimanov skeleton"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
[Date "2024.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B48"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov Variation"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ SYSTEM. Learn the squares rather than a rigid move tree: pawns c5(traded)/e6/a6/b5, Qc7, Nc6, Bb7 and often Rc8, with Nf6, Be7 and castling added when tactics allow. The d-pawn usually stays home in the opening, preserving flexibility. Aman's practical heuristic is to develop first on the wing opposite White's castled king. These are destinations, not permission to ignore White's threats. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { The speedrun's default. The c5/e6 pawn pair defines the repertoire, though anti-Sicilians can change the follow-up. } 3. d4 cxd4 { Capture when White cannot recapture with a pawn - i.e. when the recapture will be Nxd4 or Qxd4. If a c3-pawn still supports d4, taking hands White a broad centre, which is exactly why the Alapin needs a different treatment. } 4. Nxd4 Nc6 { In the main Open Sicilian move order: knight before queen, queen before a-pawn. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 { Qc7 is the speedrun's main move before ...a6. If White jumps Nb5, Qb8 followed by ...a6 is often available, but verify the concrete position. } 6. Be2 a6 { Now ...a6 prepares ...b5 and controls b5. } 7. O-O b5 { White has castled short, so Black begins queenside development and expansion. } 8. Bf3 Bb7 { ...b5 and ...Bb7 are a package - the point of the pawn move is to open the long diagonal. Typical follow-ups include ...Rc8, ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...O-O, but their order is tactical. } *

[Event "02 Recapture rules on c6"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
[Date "2024.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B48"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov Variation"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ PRACTICAL RECAPTURE GUIDE from the series. Prefer ...Qxc6 when the queen is already on c7 and the recapture is tactically safe; it preserves the pawn structure and builds the queen-bishop battery. If the queen is not ready, ...bxc6 is the usual structural choice, often followed quickly by ...d5; after exd5, the c-pawn commonly recaptures. ...dxc6 is an exception justified by a concrete tactical or structural reason, not the default. Always check whether a bishop recapture or an immediate e4-e5 changes the calculation. The same preservation principle appears after ...Ne5-c4 Bxc4: ...Qxc4 usually keeps the c-file and queenside pawns healthier than ...bxc4. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Be2 b5 8. O-O Bb7 9. Nxc6 { THE DECISION POINT - one of the series' most repeated themes. } 9... Qxc6 { The speedrun's thematic recapture: preserve the pawn structure and place the queen beside the b7-bishop. In a real game compare ...Qxc6 with ...Bxc6 before moving. } 10. Bf3 Rc8 { Keep pressure on the c-file, but do not continue mechanically: White's e4-e5 break must now be calculated. } *

[Event "03 English Attack, Be3 and Qd2"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
[Date "2024.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B48"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov, English Attack"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ vs the English Attack. If White plays f3 and g4, meet g4 with ...h6 first: after g5 hxg5 White must recapture with a piece, the g-file opens toward his own king, and h4 no longer renews the threat. ...d6 is the solidifier against the f3/g4 storm - after g5 the knight drops to d7 and the ...Ne5/...Nc4 counterplay is untouched. Hunt the dark squares around the king with ...Qa5, ...Bb4 and ...Bxc3. Warning: ...Bxa3 is overrated - check Ndxb5 and Ncxb5 before playing it. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 { Qd2 announces queenside castling. Switch the order: kingside pieces first, then the usual queenside expansion. } 7... Nf6 { The point is that White must now spend a move stopping ...Ng4. If he does not, take the invitation - trading a knight for the dark-squared bishop guts the English Attack. } 8. f3 (8. O-O-O Ng4 { White castled long without covering g4, so this comes with force. } 9. Nxc6 Nxe3 10. Qxe3 Qxc6 { Queen recapture here because White wants e4-e5, and it keeps the b-pawn free for the assault. } 11. f3 b5 { Now the race. ...b5, ...b4 hitting the c3-knight, then ...a5-a4-a3. Once ...a3 lands White has no threats left. } 12. Kb1 b4 13. Nd5 { Unsound. } 13... Bc5 { Hits the queen on e3 and forces her off the file before White can consolidate the outpost. }) 8... Be7 9. O-O-O b5 10. Nxc6 Qxc6 11. e5 { The KNeres post-game position. White attacks the f6-knight, but Black should look for a counter-threat before retreating. } 11... b4 { The key Taimanov idea. Attack the c3-knight; if it moves, d5 becomes available to Black's f6-knight. The move itself is not check. } 12. exf6 { If White carries out the original threat, Black continues rather than panicking. } 12... bxc3 { The pawn attacks the queen on d2. Continuing with a casual capture would allow ...cxd2+, winning the queen with check. } 13. Qxc3 Qxc3 14. bxc3 Bxf6 { The queens come off and Black recovers the f6-pawn with comfortable play. } *

