[Variant "Standard"]

{ vs quiet development. Against Bd3 the reply is ...Ne5 - the perfect pivot square: it hits the bishop, prepares ...Nc4, keeps ...Ng4 in reserve. Then ...Nc4 forces Bxc4 and you recapture Qxc4, NEVER bxc4, which wrecks the chain and shuts the c-file where all your play lives. Two conditions on the ...Ne5-c4 jump: it is strongest with White's queen on d2 (forking queen and bishop) and pointless with the queen on e2; and with a White knight on b3 it allows Na5, so insert ...b4 first. Do not trade on d3 - cxd3 repairs White's structure and opens his c-file. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. Be2 { Soft: it neither stops ...Ng4 nor attacks anything. } 8... Bb4 { So hit e4 immediately. } 9. O-O Nxe4 { Winning a clean central pawn. } 10. Qd3 Nxc3 11. bxc3 Be7 { Black has won a central pawn and damaged White's structure. This is a clear practical advantage, not an automatic win. } *

[Event "05 The Nxc6 bxc6 structures"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
[Date "2024.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B47"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov Variation"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ In these positions the king is genuinely safe on e8 - castling short can invite Qg4 and Bh6, so leaving the king in the centre is a real option. Push ...a5-a4 to stop b3 and deny White the queenside consolidation. ...Nd7-c5 hits the d3-bishop, then ...Ba6 offers the trade of your problem bishop for White's best defender - a move that almost always works here. A slow but permanent plan: ...Qb6 and the knight reroute Na6-b4 hitting a2 forever. Budget clock for it: this plan takes many moves and losing on time is the real danger, not the position. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Nxc6 bxc6 { The queen is on c7 but the b-pawn is still home; here White has no immediate e5, so the solid recapture is fine. } 7. Bd3 d5 { Immediately. The doubled c-pawn exists to support this break, backed by both the c- and e-pawns. } 8. O-O Nf6 9. Qe2 Be7 10. e5 { Space. } 10... Nd7 { The knight simply drops back - it always needs a square, and if it is ever forced to g8 you have mishandled the opening. } 11. f4 c5 { The pawn mass rolls. } *

