{ vs the Maroczy. Recapture rule flips here: after Nxc6 take bxc6 if ...Bc5 is not yet played, but dxc6 once it is - after ...bxc6 White plays e5 and the knight has no square, while after ...dxc6, e5 runs into ...Ng4 hitting e5 and f2. If White plays e5 in these structures ...Ng4 is the standard hit. If he develops quietly instead, play ...e5 yourself and follow with ...Ke7, ...Be6, ...h6, ...Rd8, ...a5, ...Bb4. In queenless positions here the king is actively placed on e7. A perpetual is a floor, not a failure. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. c4 { The one line that breaks the system: c4 stops ...b5, so the ...Qc7/...a6/...b5/...Bb7 machine does not run. } 5... Nf6 { Hit e4 at once instead. } 6. Nc3 Bc5 { Hitting the d4-knight. } 7. Be3 Qb6 { Hits d4 and b2 at the same time. If White drops the knight he loses material; if he moves it, trade on e3 and collect b2. } 8. Na4 { Hitting queen and bishop while covering b2. } 8... Bb4+ { White has no comfortable answer: Nc3 loses a pawn, Bd2 leaves the d4-knight hanging, and Ke2 forfeits castling. } 9. Bd2 Qxd4 { Take the knight immediately. Retreating the queen would throw away the point of the tactic. } *

