{ vs Bc4. He rates this as the best Sicilian against the Bowdler. The pawn on e6 kills the f7 mate and takes d5 from the bishop, so the entire scholar's-mate scheme that motivates Bc4 is dead on arrival - you cannot be mated on f7 in this opening. Conditions on the trap: ...c4 only works against Bb3. If White retreats to d3 instead, or has already committed a pawn to d3 covering c4, forget it and play the normal setup. If White meets ...a6 with a4, settle for ...b6 and ...Bb7 - nearly the same position. And stop hunting for a tactic against a bishop parked on c4 or b3: outside the ...b5/...c4 clamp there isn't one. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bc4 { Roughly six games in ten at club level - and never mentioned in the theory books. } 3... a6 { Against Bc4 the order changes: PAWNS BEFORE PIECES. ...a6 rules out Bb5 and prepares ...b5. Bringing ...Nc6 out first is the mistake. } 4. Nc3 b5 { Hits the bishop with tempo and takes queenside space. } 5. Bb3 { The natural retreat - and an awkward one. } 5... c4 { The bishop is forced to a4 and becomes badly restricted. This is a powerful clamp, but not a literal trap: Ba4 remains legal. } *

