{ vs the Alapin. The rule is about what the move attacks, not the move number: after 1.e4 c5 2.c3 answer 2...Nf6, and after 2.Nf3 e6 3.c3 answer 3...Nf6. Club players then routinely play d4 anyway and hang e4 - check for it every single game, because ...Nxe4 is often just free. The Qa4+ fork people fear does not exist, because Black's d-pawn has not moved and the diagonal is blocked. In these IQP-flavoured structures choose ...b6 and ...Bb7 over ...a6 and ...b5, which only invites a4. } 1. e4 c5 2. c3 { c3 means d4 is coming. ABANDON the Taimanov formation - this is the strongest never-do-X in the whole series. } 2... Nf6 { Hit e4 instead. If White gets d4 in with a free hand, Black's normal setup is genuinely bad: the c-file opens against Qc7 and the c6-knight gets kicked around. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { White gains space; the knight must move again. Retreat 4...Nc7, not 4...Nb6 - from b6 it has no future, from c7 it supports ...d6, ...b5 and ...Ne6. } 4... Nc7 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nf3 d6 { Undermine the e5-wedge at once; on exd6 recapture with the e-pawn to undouble. } 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. O-O dxe5 { Remove the advanced pawn while it is available. The familiar ...e6 setup can return later, but recovering the centre comes first. } *

