{ vs an early c4. Playing the full Taimanov setup here leaves Black solid but with no aggressive options. Note the difference from 5.c4 (the Maroczy chapter), where Black is already committed to the Open Sicilian structure and adapts instead. Aman also plays this structure with White via 1.c4 specifically so you learn where the pieces go from both sides. } 1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 g6 { The one place the Taimanov setup is simply wrong. c4 weakens the dark squares, so fianchetto instead of playing ...Be7 on autopilot. } 4. g3 Bg7 5. Bg2 e6 6. O-O Nge7 { Not ...Nf6. The fianchetto controls e5 and f4 far better and keeps White's f4-f5 plan under control, while ...b5 counterplay is still available. } *

