{ vs f4. With a pawn already on f4, e4-e5 is more annoying than usual, so ...d6 is one of the clear cases for moving the d-pawn. f4 also takes e5 from your knight: route it ...Na5-c4 instead of ...Ne5. In the e5-wedge structures develop the knight via h6 to f5 and walk the king by hand with ...Kf8-g8 - castling is not required. Once White commits to f4 the g3 square is chronically weak: ...h5, ...h4 and ...Nh5-g3 is a very natural attacking scheme. } 1. e4 c5 2. f4 e6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Qc7 { So that Bxc6 must be met with the queen, keeping the structure intact and putting her on the long diagonal. } 5. Bxc6 Qxc6 6. d3 d5 7. e5 d4 { Grabbing space and clearing the a8-h1 diagonal for queen and bishop. } 8. Nbd2 Ne7 9. Ne4 Nf5 *

