{ vs Nb3 and b3/Bb2. The standing rule: never surrender your dark-squared bishop here, and never play ...b5 before White has committed the b1-knight - with the knight still home, a4 hits much harder because White answers ...b4 with Nbd2 in one move. Against b3/Bb2 systems play ...a6 early as a matter of course, even when ...d5 is screaming to be played: White's whole scheme is Bb5xc6 doubling your pawns. Against the b2-bishop the strategic goal is ...d4, slamming the long diagonal shut. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Nb3 { Now do NOT rush ...Bb7 - it runs into ideas that pressure Black into parting with the dark-squared bishop. } 6... Nf6 7. Be3 b5 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O d6 { ...Nf6, ...Qc7 and ...d6 instead of the automatic ...Bb7. Preserve the dark-squared bishop whenever practical. } 10. g4 Ne5 { Meet the kingside expansion in the centre first. ...b4 or ...Rb8 may follow, but the immediate rook move is too slow here. } *
